    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-28074",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=-3(x+2)(x+1)(x-1)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a negative cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be negative:\n\n$$\n-3(x+2)(x+1)(x-1)=-3x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=-3{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is given in the factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are  $x=-2,$ $x=-1,$ and $x=1.$ \n\nWe now find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3(0+2)(0+1)(0-1) \\\\  & =-3(2)(1)(-1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,-1,1$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=6,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250730/assets/472cf0dd7807c15e8e8334a163d1cfbfbbdcd974534e99b94222929048e72b31.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
